Z||000012||jelly2m8||12-07-2001||05:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Howard_Sacks:<BR><B>These are old cars.  I wouldn't drive that fast for an extended period of time in them.  I know from experience. I drove from exit 4 of the NJ turnpike (philly) to the George Washington bridge (new york) in under 45 min and my 86 was not the same afterwords. (Everyone is going to ask but, oil was synth with only 1000miles on it changed 5 weeks prior to land speed record :-))<P>btw, I'm not too crazy, the roads were clear and it was 3AM.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you don't trust your car, then don't drive it fast.<P> My car is probably like alot of Fiero owners are. It is well maintained, every little thing that needs replacing, has been done. <P> I can't say for other people , but I do know my 87 GT inside and out ( yes I've had the motor, and transaxle apart to see what the condition of them are inside). I doub't there isn't 1 single part I didn't have off of the car yet. Well, I just lied, I know that the heater box hasn't been out.....<P> I trust my 87 to go anywhere in it, any distance at any speed.<P> If my car breaks, or isn't the same after high speed driving, or aggressive driving, well then something was wrong with it, and it needed to be fixed anyhow.<P> When Skitime drove my V8, I told him to drive it as his own, if something happens to it, it's no good.<P> Age has nothing to do with the mechanical fitness of a well maintained car.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
